didactic verse. His work reflects the era's intellectual currents, blending detailed scientific observation with poetic form, thereby contributing uniquely to the transmission of ancient toxicology and zoology.

His most celebrated works are two surviving didactic poems: *Theriaca* and *Alexipharmaca*. *Theriaca* meticulously describes venomous animals—snakes, spiders, scorpions—their bites, and the associated symptoms, alongside remedies derived from traditional folklore and empirical observation. *Alexipharmaca*, conversely, focuses on various poisons, their effects on the human body, and an extensive catalogue of antidotes. These poems are not merely scientific treatises; they are crafted in hexameter verse, showcasing Nicander's literary skill and adherence to a long-standing Greek tradition of instructional poetry, making complex medical information accessible and memorable.

Nicander's legacy extended far beyond his lifetime, profoundly influencing later generations of physicians, pharmacologists, and poets. His detailed descriptions and systematic classification of toxins and their treatments were consulted by major figures such as Galen and Dioscorides, becoming foundational texts in ancient and medieval medicine. Roman poets like Virgil and Ovid also drew inspiration from his didactic style and natural history content, demonstrating his impact on the broader literary landscape. His work served as a crucial conduit for preserving and transmitting a vast body of empirical and theoretical knowledge concerning venoms and poisons, which remained relevant for centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-19T22:29:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#26129108692", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "249 BCE, Colophon"}, {"title": "Rise of print media and public discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "249 BCE, Colophon"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing of Hellenistic scholarship and the intellectual environment of the Library of Alexandria fostered interdisciplinary studies, blending science and literature.", "The established tradition of didactic poetry in Greek literature provided a recognized genre for Nicander to convey complex medical and scientific information.", "The practical necessity of understanding and treating venomous bites and poisons in the ancient Mediterranean spurred the accumulation of pharmacological knowledge."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-05-19T22:29:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#26129108692", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped public opinion and historical record", "type": "Idea", "year": "169 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "169